    <div class="title"><strong>About the ID-VET Project</strong></div>
        <hr />
    <p>ID-Vet addressed two critical educational priorities: inclusion and diversity and digital transformation. The project focused on vocational schools and employed a comprehensive approach that tackled both priorities simultaneously. It successfully promoted digital transformation, helped prevent early school leaving, and strengthened inclusion, equality, and non-discrimination in VET schools by reducing dropout rates, improving student well-being, developing digital skills and competences, and creating more inclusive and diverse learning environments for VET students across Europe.
    <hr/>
<p><strong>Objectives</strong></p>

The main objective of the project was to create a question framework for identifying barriers to inclusion and diversity in vocational education and training (VET) schools. The project aimed to identify and evaluate effective questions across five different European VET schools. Since many VET schools struggle to address inclusion and diversity barriers—especially for students with fewer opportunities—our approach focused on asking students directly. This method provided more accurate and actionable feedback, which helped schools implement meaningful changes and improve inclusiveness.
        <hr />
<p><strong>Implementation</strong></p>

During the implementation phase, we built capacity among partners and developed a validated set of questions to address inclusion and diversity barriers. These questions were tested across five European vocational schools.
We collected over 75,000 data points through micro-surveys, which allowed us to gain valuable insights into key barriers and identify best practices for overcoming them.
The organizations StudentPulse and Xarxa FP actively participated throughout the project. Together, we produced a whitepaper summarizing the findings and hosted a webinar to share results and provide guidance for schools on how to adopt effective survey questions and take concrete steps toward improving inclusion and diversity.
        <hr />
<p><strong>Results</strong></p>

The project resulted in a validated set of survey questions that vocational schools can now use to identify and address inclusion and diversity barriers. It also enhanced the capacity and collaboration among participating partners and demonstrated how digital tools and data-driven approaches can be effectively used to understand and remove barriers to inclusion.
Ultimately, the project had a lasting impact on vocational education in Europe and beyond by promoting digital transformation and fostering more inclusive, equitable, and diverse learning environments for all students.</p>
